# Generated by every pipeline run. Not committed, for three reasons:
#
#   1. They are private. An evidence pack holds screenshots, raw response headers and captured page
#      content, which is client detail rather than project source. The build prompt's rule is that a
#      public-safe summary and the private evidence stay separate, and an evidence pack must never
#      reach a public repository.
#   2. They are output, not input. Every run rewrites them from the source that IS committed.
#   3. They are large. A single full-tier run against a 56-page site produced 62 MB, mostly
#      screenshots. Committing that per run makes the repository unusable within a month, and git
#      history keeps it forever.
#
# Everything else under compliance/ is committed on purpose: the profiles, the registries, the
# approvals, the exceptions and the manual review records are the inputs and the decisions, and they
# are exactly what a later reader needs.
evidence/
reports/
releases/

# Also generated, and less obviously so. The manual accessibility checklist is rewritten on every
# run and stamped with the commit under review, so committing it makes the working tree dirty again
# the moment the next run happens — which means AGENCY-PROVENANCE-001, the control that asks whether
# the code tested is the code the commit identifies, could never pass on any project. A control that
# can never pass is as useless as one that always does.
#
# The INPUT here is manual-review.yml, the record of what a person actually examined. That is
# committed. The checklist is the blank form it gets copied out of.
accessibility/manual-checklist.md

# Keep an approved evidence pack by copying it out of releases/ deliberately, not by un-ignoring
# this. A pack worth keeping is one somebody chose to keep.
